insert into questions (id, unit, topic, concept, stem, choices, answer, why) values
  ('CI01', 'Corporate Issuers', 'Corporate Governance', 'Board independence', 'A board of directors is considered more independent, and better positioned to monitor management objectively, when it has a higher proportion of directors who:', '["Also serve as senior executives of the company","Have no material business or personal relationships with the company or its management","Were personally nominated by the chief executive officer"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Independent directors have no material financial, employment, or personal ties to the company or its management beyond board service, which lets them monitor management objectively rather than defer to it. Directors who are also company executives are insiders by definition, and a director nominated by the CEO is more likely to defer in practice, even though nomination alone does not automatically create a disqualifying relationship the way a material business tie does.'),
  ('CI02', 'Corporate Issuers', 'Corporate Governance', 'Cumulative voting', 'Compared with statutory (straight) voting for the election of directors, cumulative voting is generally considered more favorable to:', '["Majority shareholders, because it concentrates all of their votes on a single favored nominee","Minority shareholders, because it lets them concentrate their votes on fewer board seats and improve their odds of electing at least one director","Creditors, because it gives bondholders a proportional say in board elections"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Cumulative voting lets a shareholder cast all of their available votes (shares held times open board seats) for a single candidate, letting minority shareholders concentrate their votes and win board representation they could never achieve under one-vote-per-share-per-seat statutory voting. Majority shareholders already control enough votes to win every seat under either system, so cumulative voting mainly protects minority shareholders rather than the majority, and bondholders have no voting rights in director elections at all.'),
  ('CI03', 'Corporate Issuers', 'Corporate Governance', 'Pyramid ownership structure', 'In a pyramid ownership structure, a controlling family holds a majority stake in a holding company, which in turn holds a majority stake in an operating company. This structure most directly allows the family to:', '["Control the operating company''s votes while owning a much smaller share of its underlying cash flow rights","Eliminate all agency conflicts between itself and minority shareholders of the operating company","Guarantee that its economic ownership and voting control in the operating company are identical"]'::jsonb, 0, 'A pyramid structure lets a controlling family multiply its voting control down each layer while its actual cash-flow claim on the bottom-tier operating company shrinks with each additional layer of less-than-100% ownership, creating a wide gap between control and economic ownership. Far from eliminating agency conflicts, this wedge between control and cash-flow rights is exactly what risks the family extracting value at the expense of the operating company''s minority shareholders, and it guarantees a divergence between ownership and control, not equality.'),
  ('CI04', 'Corporate Issuers', 'Corporate Governance', 'Dual-class rationale', 'A common justification founders give for adopting a dual-class share structure at an initial public offering is that it:', '["Equalizes voting power across all shareholders regardless of how many shares each holds","Is required by securities regulators for any company with more than one class of assets","Allows founders to raise external equity capital while retaining voting control and pursuing a long-term strategy without short-term shareholder pressure"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Dual-class structures let founders sell economic interests to public investors to raise capital while keeping superior-vote shares for themselves, insulating their long-term strategic decisions from short-term public-shareholder pressure. This is the opposite of equalizing voting power, which is precisely what a dual-class structure abandons, and no regulator requires the structure -- it is a voluntary choice made at listing.'),
  ('CI05', 'Corporate Issuers', 'Corporate Governance', 'Say-on-pay vote', 'A say-on-pay shareholder vote on executive compensation is best described as:', '["Legally binding on the board, which must adopt whatever compensation plan receives majority shareholder support","Typically non-binding and advisory, expressing shareholder sentiment without legally compelling the board to act","A vote held only when a company is being acquired"]'::jsonb, 1, 'In most jurisdictions a say-on-pay vote is advisory: it signals shareholder approval or disapproval of executive pay practices, and a strong against vote creates reputational and governance pressure on the board, but it does not legally force a specific change. Treating it as binding overstates its formal legal force, and say-on-pay votes recur on a regular cycle (often annually) rather than being tied only to a merger or acquisition event.'),
  ('CI06', 'Corporate Issuers', 'Corporate Governance', 'One-tier vs two-tier board', 'Relative to a one-tier (unitary) board structure, a two-tier board structure is distinguished by:', '["Combining a supervisory function and a management function into a single group of directors that both sets strategy and runs daily operations","Having no non-executive or independent directors at all","Separating a supervisory board of non-executive and independent directors from a management board of executives who run daily operations"]'::jsonb, 2, 'A two-tier structure formally separates a supervisory board, typically composed largely of independent, non-executive directors, from a management board of executives who run the company day to day. Combining both functions into one group of directors instead describes the one-tier structure, and a two-tier system does not eliminate independent directors -- the supervisory board is where most of them sit.'),
  ('CI07', 'Corporate Issuers', 'Corporate Governance', 'Cross-shareholdings', 'Two companies that each own a meaningful equity stake in the other, without either being fully consolidated, are best described as having:', '["A dual-class share structure","Cross-shareholdings, which can entrench incumbent management at both companies against outside shareholder pressure","A two-tier board structure"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Cross-shareholdings, in which each company holds a stake in the other, create mutually supportive blocks of stock that make it harder for outside shareholders at either company to challenge incumbent management, since each company''s votes tend to back the other''s status quo. This is distinct from a dual-class structure, which separates voting and cash-flow rights within a single company''s own shares, and from one-tier versus two-tier board choices, which describe how a single company''s board is organized rather than how two companies hold stakes in each other.'),
  ('CI08', 'Corporate Issuers', 'Corporate Governance', 'Dual-class voting power', 'A company has 100 million total shares outstanding: 10 million Class B shares, each carrying 10 votes, held entirely by the founder, and 90 million Class A shares, each carrying 1 vote, held by public shareholders. The founder''s percentage of total voting power is closest to:', '["10.0%","100.0%","52.6%"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Total votes equal (10 million x 10) plus (90 million x 1), or 190 million, and the founder controls 100 million of them, 52.6% -- a voting majority from just 10% of the shares outstanding. The 10.0% figure is the founder''s share of shares outstanding (economic ownership), exactly the gap a dual-class structure is designed to create, and 100.0% would hold only if the 90 million Class A shares carried no votes at all.'),
  ('CI09', 'Corporate Issuers', 'Stakeholder Management', 'Stakeholder vs shareholder theory', 'Relative to shareholder theory, stakeholder theory holds that a corporation''s managers should:', '["Maximize only shareholder wealth, since shareholders bear the ultimate residual risk of the firm","Prioritize creditor interests above all other claimants, since creditors are paid before shareholders","Balance the interests of a broader group, including employees, customers, suppliers, and communities, alongside shareholders"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Stakeholder theory holds that a firm exists to serve, and should balance the interests of, all parties with a stake in its outcomes -- employees, customers, suppliers, communities, and shareholders alike -- rather than optimizing for any single group. Maximizing only shareholder wealth describes shareholder theory instead, the narrower, competing view stakeholder theory is contrasted against, and no mainstream governance framework elevates creditors above every other claimant as a general rule.'),
  ('CI10', 'Corporate Issuers', 'Stakeholder Management', 'Shareholder-creditor conflict', 'A firm''s shareholders push management to take on a much riskier new project financed with the firm''s existing asset base, without offering existing bondholders any additional compensation. This scenario most directly illustrates a conflict between:', '["Shareholders and creditors, because riskier projects can transfer value from bondholders to shareholders without repricing the debt","Shareholders and customers, because riskier projects always reduce product quality","Two classes of shareholders, because only common shareholders benefit from risk-taking"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Increasing asset risk after debt is already priced and outstanding can shift value from creditors to shareholders, since shareholders capture the upside of a risky bet while creditors bear more downside risk without being compensated through a higher promised yield -- the classic shareholder-creditor agency conflict, sometimes called asset substitution. This scenario says nothing about product quality or about differing classes of shareholders; it is specifically about fixed-claim (debt) holders versus residual-claim (equity) holders.'),
  ('CI11', 'Corporate Issuers', 'Stakeholder Management', 'Management empire building', 'A CEO pursues a large corporate acquisition primarily to increase the size and prestige of the company under his control, even though independent analysis suggests the deal will not create value for shareholders. This behavior is most closely associated with:', '["Cumulative voting, which allows minority shareholders to block such acquisitions","Regulation limiting the size of any single acquisition a company may complete","The free cash flow hypothesis, in which managers with excess cash overinvest in low-return, empire-building growth rather than returning cash to shareholders"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Empire building for its own sake, rather than for shareholder value, is the textbook manifestation of the free cash flow hypothesis: managers with cash and discretion tend to overinvest in growth, including value-destroying acquisitions, rather than pay it out, because a bigger company usually means more managerial power, pay, and prestige. Cumulative voting affects director elections, not acquisition approval directly, and there is no general regulation capping deal size based on company preference alone.'),
  ('CI12', 'Corporate Issuers', 'Stakeholder Management', 'ESG governance pillar', 'In an ESG framework used to evaluate a company, board independence, executive compensation structure, and shareholder rights protections fall primarily under which pillar?', '["Environmental","Social","Governance"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Board composition, executive pay structure, and shareholder rights are core governance topics -- how the company is directed and controlled -- and are classified under the governance (G) pillar of ESG. The environmental pillar covers issues like emissions and resource use, and the social pillar covers issues like labor practices and community relations, neither of which describes board or shareholder-rights mechanics.'),
  ('CI13', 'Corporate Issuers', 'Stakeholder Management', 'ESG materiality', 'An analyst evaluating a company''s ESG profile focuses first on the environmental, social, and governance factors most likely to affect the company''s long-term financial performance and risk, rather than every possible ESG factor in general. This approach reflects the concept of:', '["ESG materiality, since not all ESG factors are equally relevant to every company''s value and risk","Greenwashing, since it selectively reports only favorable ESG data","Negative screening, since it excludes entire industries from consideration"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Materiality in an ESG context means recognizing that relevant factors differ by industry and company -- water usage is highly material for a beverage company but far less so for a software firm -- so a rigorous analysis prioritizes the factors most likely to affect financial performance and risk rather than treating every ESG metric as equally important. Greenwashing refers to a company misleadingly overstating its own ESG credentials, and negative screening is a portfolio-construction technique that excludes whole sectors or companies, neither of which describes an analyst''s own prioritization process.'),
  ('CI14', 'Corporate Issuers', 'Stakeholder Management', 'Controlling vs minority shareholders', 'A controlling shareholder causes a company to purchase assets from another business the controlling shareholder personally owns, at a price above fair market value. This scenario most directly illustrates a conflict between:', '["The company and its regulators, since related-party transactions are always illegal","Creditors and the board of directors, since only lenders are harmed by overpaying for assets","Controlling and minority shareholders, since the controlling shareholder can extract personal value through a related-party transaction at minority shareholders'' expense"]'::jsonb, 2, 'This is a related-party transaction that lets a controlling shareholder personally benefit at an inflated price, directly at the expense of the company and therefore its minority shareholders, a core example of the controlling-versus-minority-shareholder conflict that concentrated-ownership structures can enable. Related-party transactions are not automatically illegal -- many jurisdictions permit them if properly disclosed and approved by independent directors -- and the harm here falls on minority shareholders specifically, not primarily on creditors or regulators.'),
  ('CI15', 'Corporate Issuers', 'Stakeholder Management', 'Employee vs shareholder trade-off', 'A company facing a cash shortfall must choose between cutting its workforce or reducing its dividend to shareholders. This decision illustrates that corporate decisions:', '["Never actually involve trade-offs, since maximizing shareholder value always also maximizes employee welfare","Often require managers to weigh the competing interests of different stakeholder groups against one another","Should be resolved solely according to which stakeholder group has a formal seat on the board"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Real corporate decisions frequently pit one stakeholder group''s interests against another''s, exactly as in this cash-shortfall example, and managers must weigh these competing claims rather than assume they always align. Shareholder value and employee welfare do not automatically move together, which is precisely why this kind of trade-off exists, and formal board representation is not the deciding principle for resolving stakeholder conflicts -- most stakeholder groups, including employees in most jurisdictions, have no board seat at all yet still have real, legitimate interests at stake.'),
  ('CI16', 'Corporate Issuers', 'Business Models', 'Value creation vs capture', 'A company invents a breakthrough technology that generates enormous benefits for its customers, but competitors quickly copy the technology and drive prices down to the point where the inventing company earns minimal profit from it. This outcome best illustrates that a business model must do more than create value; it must also:', '["Avoid all competition permanently once a product is invented","Capture a sustainable share of the value it creates, typically through some form of competitive protection","Rely exclusively on patents, since patents are the only mechanism that allows value capture"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Creating value for customers is necessary but not sufficient for a business model to succeed financially; the firm must also capture a durable share of that value, usually through some barrier to imitation such as brand, scale, network effects, or intellectual property, or competitors will compete the profit away exactly as in this example. No business avoids competition forever, and patents are only one of several possible mechanisms for value capture -- brand strength, switching costs, and network effects can each protect value capture just as effectively.'),
  ('CI17', 'Corporate Issuers', 'Business Models', 'Business model components', 'A complete description of a company''s business model would most usefully include all of the following EXCEPT:', '["Its value proposition, target customers, and how it generates revenue","Its key resources, activities, and cost structure needed to deliver its offering","The specific day-to-day trading price of its publicly listed shares"]'::jsonb, 2, 'A business model describes how a firm creates, delivers, and captures value: its value proposition, customer segments, revenue model, and the resources, activities, and costs required to operate -- all internal, structural questions about how the firm works. A stock''s daily trading price is an external market outcome that reflects how investors value the company at a point in time; it says nothing about the underlying mechanics of how the business itself creates or captures value.'),
  ('CI18', 'Corporate Issuers', 'Business Models', 'Revenue model design', 'A software company shifts from selling one-time perpetual licenses to charging customers a recurring monthly subscription fee. This change primarily affects which component of its business model?', '["Its value proposition, since the underlying software features have changed","Its revenue model, since it now captures value through recurring payments rather than a single upfront payment","Its target customer segment, since only new customers can be reached through subscriptions"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Switching from a one-time license fee to a recurring subscription changes how and when the company collects payment for the same underlying product -- a change to its revenue model, one of the core components of a business model. The software''s features and value proposition need not change at all for this shift to occur, and a subscription structure can be sold to existing customers just as easily as new ones; it does not by itself redefine who the target customer is.'),
  ('CI19', 'Corporate Issuers', 'Business Models', 'Network effects', 'A two-sided online marketplace becomes more valuable to each new buyer as more sellers join the platform, and more valuable to each new seller as more buyers join. This dynamic, which can create a strong and self-reinforcing competitive advantage, is best described as:', '["Vertical integration, where the platform owns every stage of its supply chain","A cost leadership strategy, where the platform simply charges the lowest price in the market","A network effect, where the platform''s value to each user increases as more users of either side join"]'::jsonb, 2, 'This mutually reinforcing dynamic, where added users on one side of the platform increase the value delivered to users on the other side, is a network effect, and it is a powerful competitive advantage precisely because it becomes harder for a smaller rival to replicate as the leading platform grows. It has nothing to do with owning supply-chain stages, which is vertical integration, and nothing here is about being the low-price provider, which is a cost leadership strategy describing how a firm competes on price rather than how a platform''s value scales with its user base.'),
  ('CI20', 'Corporate Issuers', 'Business Models', 'Operating leverage', 'A firm with a business model built around very high fixed costs (for example, a large fixed manufacturing base) and comparatively low variable costs per unit will see its operating profit margin:', '["Stay constant regardless of sales volume, since fixed costs by definition never change the margin","Rise sharply as sales volume increases, because fixed costs are spread over more units while variable costs per unit stay roughly constant","Fall as sales volume increases, because more units sold always increases total variable costs faster than revenue"]'::jsonb, 1, 'When most costs are fixed, each additional unit sold spreads that fixed cost base over a larger volume while adding relatively little variable cost, so operating margin expands as volume grows -- a form of operating leverage built into the business model''s cost structure. Margins are not constant under a high-fixed-cost model; that describes a model with mostly variable costs instead, and total variable cost rising with volume does not itself shrink margins, since revenue rises alongside it at a roughly constant rate per unit.'),
  ('CI21', 'Corporate Issuers', 'Business Models', 'Key resources and moat', 'Two firms sell nearly identical consumer products at similar prices, but one firm consistently earns higher returns because customers have grown strongly loyal to its brand over decades and are reluctant to switch. In business model terms, this brand loyalty functions as:', '["A key resource that supports a durable competitive advantage and helps the firm capture more of the value it creates","A component of revenue model design, since it changes how the firm bills customers","Evidence that the firm has the lower cost structure of the two"]'::jsonb, 0, 'A hard-to-replicate asset like decades of brand loyalty is a key resource that gives a firm a durable competitive advantage, letting it capture a larger, more protected share of the value it creates even when its underlying product is similar to a rival''s. Brand loyalty does not describe how the firm bills customers, which is a revenue model question, and nothing in the scenario says anything about relative costs -- the advantage described here is about customer willingness to pay and stay, not about production efficiency.'),
  ('CI22', 'Corporate Issuers', 'Capital Budgeting', 'NPV decision rule', 'Using the net present value (NPV) decision rule for a single, independent capital investment project, a company should:', '["Accept the project only if its internal rate of return equals exactly its NPV","Reject the project if its NPV is positive, since a positive NPV signals excess risk","Accept the project only if its NPV is greater than zero"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The NPV decision rule accepts an independent project whenever its NPV is positive, since a positive NPV means the project''s discounted cash inflows exceed its cost and is expected to add value for shareholders; a project with an NPV of exactly zero is marginally acceptable, and any negative-NPV project should be rejected. IRR and NPV are two different outputs of a discounted cash flow analysis, not values that must equal each other, and a positive NPV reflects expected value creation, not a warning sign of risk.'),
  ('CI23', 'Corporate Issuers', 'Capital Budgeting', 'NPV vs IRR conflict', 'Two mutually exclusive projects differ substantially in scale and in the timing of their cash flows. The NPV decision rule ranks Project A higher, while the IRR decision rule ranks Project B higher. In this situation, an analyst should generally rely on:', '["The IRR ranking, because IRR is expressed as a percentage and is therefore always comparable across projects of different scale","The NPV ranking, because NPV directly measures the expected dollar increase in firm value and avoids IRR''s reinvestment-rate assumption problems","Whichever ranking uses the higher discount rate, since a higher discount rate is always more conservative"]'::jsonb, 1, 'When NPV and IRR disagree for mutually exclusive projects, most often because of differences in scale or the timing of cash flows, NPV is the more reliable rule because it directly measures the dollar increase in firm value and, unlike IRR, does not implicitly assume interim cash flows are reinvested at the project''s own IRR. IRR''s percentage form is exactly what makes it misleading when comparing projects of different sizes, since a small project can post a very high IRR on a trivial dollar amount, and the choice between the two rules has nothing to do with which analysis used a higher discount rate.'),
  ('CI24', 'Corporate Issuers', 'Capital Budgeting', 'Sunk cost identification', 'A manufacturer spent 200,000 dollars two years ago developing a prototype for a new product; that amount has already been paid and cannot be recovered regardless of what the company decides now. In evaluating whether to proceed with full production today, this 200,000 dollars should be treated as:', '["An incremental cash outflow that reduces the project''s NPV","An opportunity cost that must be added to the initial investment","A sunk cost that is irrelevant to the current accept-or-reject decision"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Money already spent and unrecoverable regardless of today''s decision is a sunk cost, and sunk costs are irrelevant to a forward-looking capital budgeting decision because they do not change based on which choice is made now. Treating it as an incremental cash outflow would double-count a cost that has no bearing on the decision at hand, and it is not an opportunity cost either, since an opportunity cost reflects a forgone alternative use of a resource going forward, not money already gone.'),
  ('CI25', 'Corporate Issuers', 'Capital Budgeting', 'IRR vs required return', 'An independent capital project has an internal rate of return (IRR) of 9%, and the company''s required rate of return for projects of similar risk is 12%. Using the IRR decision rule, the company should:', '["Accept the project, because any positive IRR indicates the project is expected to add value","Reject the project, because its IRR is below the required rate of return for its risk level","Accept the project, but only after re-discounting its cash flows at the risk-free rate instead"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The IRR decision rule accepts a project only when its IRR exceeds the required rate of return (the hurdle rate) that compensates investors for the project''s risk, and here 9% falls short of the 12% hurdle, so the project should be rejected. A positive IRR alone is not sufficient if it still falls below what the project''s risk level demands, and there is no step in the IRR rule that involves re-discounting cash flows at the risk-free rate, which would understate the compensation required for a risky project.'),
  ('CI26', 'Corporate Issuers', 'Capital Budgeting', 'IRR calculation', 'A project requires an initial investment of 25,000 dollars today and is expected to generate a single cash flow of 28,000 dollars at the end of one year. The project''s internal rate of return (IRR) is closest to:', '["12.0%","112.0%","10.7%"]'::jsonb, 0, 'IRR is the discount rate that sets NPV to zero: the 3,000 dollar gain on a 25,000 dollar investment is a 3,000/25,000 = 12.0% return. The 112.0% figure comes from taking the ratio of the future cash flow to the investment (28,000/25,000 = 1.12) and forgetting to subtract the 1 to isolate the rate of growth, and 10.7% divides the same 3,000 dollar gain by the future cash flow of 28,000 instead of the initial investment of 25,000, the wrong base for a rate of return.'),
  ('CI27', 'Corporate Issuers', 'Capital Budgeting', 'NPV calculation', 'A firm invests $55,000 today in a project that will pay a single cash flow of $66,000 in one year. Using a 10% discount rate, the project''s NPV is closest to:', '["$11,000","$60,000","$5,000"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Discounting the single cash flow gives a present value of 66,000/1.10 = $60,000, and NPV subtracts the initial investment: 60,000 - 55,000 = $5,000. The $11,000 figure is the undiscounted profit (66,000 - 55,000), which ignores the time value of money entirely, and $60,000 is only the discounted cash inflow, forgetting to subtract the initial investment at all.'),
  ('CI28', 'Corporate Issuers', 'Capital Budgeting', 'Multi-period NPV', 'A project requires an initial investment of $9,000 today and is expected to generate cash flows of $5,500 at the end of year 1 and $6,050 at the end of year 2. Using a 10% discount rate, the project''s NPV is closest to:', '["$1,000","$2,550","$10,000"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Each cash flow is discounted for its own number of years: 5,500/1.10 + 6,050/1.10^2 = 5,000 + 5,000 = $10,000 in total present value, less the $9,000 initial investment, for an NPV of $1,000. The $2,550 figure simply adds the two undiscounted cash flows and subtracts the investment (5,500 + 6,050 - 9,000), ignoring the time value of money, and $10,000 is the total present value of the two cash flows alone, forgetting to subtract the initial investment at all.'),
  ('CI29', 'Corporate Issuers', 'Capital Budgeting', 'Mutually exclusive project choice', 'A firm must choose between two mutually exclusive projects, each requiring a $20,000 initial investment today. Project A will pay a single cash flow of $23,100 in one year; Project B will pay a single cash flow of $24,200 in one year. Using a 10% discount rate and the NPV decision rule, the firm should:', '["Accept Project B, because its NPV of $2,000 exceeds Project A''s NPV of $1,000","Accept Project A, because its cash flow of $23,100 is more conservative than Project B''s larger cash flow","Accept both projects, because each has a positive NPV"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Project A''s NPV is 23,100/1.10 - 20,000 = 21,000 - 20,000 = $1,000, and Project B''s NPV is 24,200/1.10 - 20,000 = 22,000 - 20,000 = $2,000, so the higher-NPV project, B, is the correct choice between two mutually exclusive options. Since the projects are mutually exclusive, accepting both is not available regardless of both having positive NPV, and nothing in the facts given supports a claim about relative conservatism -- the decision rule compares NPV, not a subjective sense of which cash flow feels safer.'),
  ('CI30', 'Corporate Issuers', 'Capital Structure', 'Tax benefit of debt', 'Relative to equity financing, debt financing offers a company a tax-related advantage primarily because:', '["Principal repayments on debt are tax-deductible, while interest payments are not","Debt is never subject to any form of taxation at the corporate level","Interest payments on debt are tax-deductible, while dividend payments to shareholders are not"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Interest expense reduces a company''s taxable income and is deductible in most tax jurisdictions, creating an interest tax shield that lowers the effective after-tax cost of debt relative to equity, whose dividend payments are made from after-tax income and are not deductible at all. Principal repayment of debt is a return of capital, not an expense, and is not tax-deductible, and debt-related interest is very much subject to the broader tax system -- the advantage is the deductibility of interest specifically, not a blanket tax exemption.'),
  ('CI31', 'Corporate Issuers', 'Capital Structure', 'Trade-off theory', 'The trade-off theory of capital structure holds that a firm''s optimal mix of debt and equity is the point at which:', '["The firm uses no debt at all, since equity carries no risk of financial distress","The firm uses the maximum amount of debt its lenders are willing to provide","The marginal tax benefit of additional debt is just offset by the marginal increase in expected costs of financial distress"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Trade-off theory holds that as a firm adds debt, it gains a growing tax shield benefit but also raises the expected costs of financial distress (including bankruptcy risk), and the optimal capital structure balances these two forces at the point where an additional dollar of debt''s tax benefit is just offset by its added distress cost. An all-equity structure ignores the valuable tax shield debt can provide, and simply maximizing debt up to a lender''s limit ignores that distress costs rise as leverage increases, eventually outweighing the tax benefit.'),
  ('CI32', 'Corporate Issuers', 'Capital Structure', 'Leverage and risk', 'Holding operating performance constant, increasing the proportion of debt in a firm''s capital structure will most likely cause the variability (risk) of its return on equity to:', '["Increase, because fixed interest obligations magnify the effect of changes in operating income on the residual amount left for shareholders","Decrease, because debt holders absorb any downside risk instead of shareholders","Stay unchanged, because financial leverage only affects the level of ROE, not its variability"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Fixed interest payments must be made regardless of how operating income performs, so any given percentage change in operating income translates into a larger percentage change in the earnings, and therefore the ROE, left over for shareholders -- financial leverage magnifies risk as well as potential return. Debt holders have a senior, fixed claim precisely so they do not absorb operating downside the way shareholders do, and leverage affects both the level and the variability of ROE, not the level alone.'),
  ('CI33', 'Corporate Issuers', 'Capital Structure', 'When leverage helps ROE', 'A firm''s financial leverage will raise its return on equity above what an all-equity structure would produce only when:', '["The firm''s operating (pre-financing) return on capital exceeds its after-tax cost of debt","The firm''s tax rate is exactly zero","The firm chooses to finance entirely with equity instead of debt"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Leverage raises ROE above the unlevered case whenever the return the firm earns on its assets exceeds what it pays, after tax, to borrow the money used to fund them, since the spread between the two accrues to shareholders on every borrowed dollar; when operating return falls below the after-tax cost of debt, that same leverage instead drags ROE below the unlevered case. A zero tax rate does not create this condition by itself, since the comparison is between operating return and the cost of debt regardless of the tax rate, and an all-equity structure is the unlevered benchmark itself, not an example of leverage helping ROE.'),
  ('CI34', 'Corporate Issuers', 'Capital Structure', 'MM capital structure irrelevance', 'Under the original Modigliani-Miller propositions, in a hypothetical world with no taxes, no bankruptcy costs, and no other market frictions, a firm''s choice of capital structure:', '["Determines the firm''s total value, since more debt always increases value in a frictionless market","Has no effect on the firm''s total value, which depends only on the earning power and risk of its underlying assets","Determines the firm''s total value only if the firm pays no dividends"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Modigliani and Miller showed that in a frictionless world with no taxes or bankruptcy costs, how a firm slices its cash flows between debt and equity holders does not change the total value of those cash flows, which depends only on the firm''s underlying assets and operations; capital structure is, in this idealized setting, irrelevant to firm value. This is precisely why the real-world factors that do make capital structure matter, like the interest tax shield and financial distress costs, are the focus of trade-off theory instead, and MM''s irrelevance result has nothing to do with a firm''s dividend policy.'),
  ('CI35', 'Corporate Issuers', 'Capital Structure', 'Leverage effect on ROE', 'Firm U and Firm L are identical in every respect except capital structure. Both have $1,000,000 in total capital and generate $120,000 in operating income (EBIT) in a given year. Firm U is financed entirely with equity. Firm L is financed with $500,000 of debt at a 5% interest rate and $500,000 of equity. Both firms face a 40% tax rate. Firm L''s return on equity (ROE) for the year is closest to:', '["14.4%","5.7%","11.4%"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Firm L''s interest expense is 500,000 x 5% = $25,000, so its taxable income is 120,000 - 25,000 = $95,000; after 40% tax, net income is $57,000, and ROE is 57,000/500,000 = 11.4% (well above Firm U''s unlevered 7.2%, since operating return here exceeds the after-tax cost of debt). The 14.4% figure forgets to deduct the $25,000 interest expense before applying tax, using the full $120,000 as if it were taxable income, and 5.7% divides net income by the firm''s $1,000,000 of total capital instead of its $500,000 of equity -- an ROA-style denominator mistake.'),
  ('CI36', 'Corporate Issuers', 'Capital Structure', 'Leverage ROE in downturn', 'Firm L has $1,000,000 in total capital, consisting of $500,000 of debt at a 5% interest rate and $500,000 of equity, and faces a 40% tax rate. In a weak year, operating income (EBIT) falls to $40,000. Firm L''s return on equity (ROE) for that year is closest to:', '["1.8%","4.8%","3.0%"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Interest expense stays fixed at 500,000 x 5% = $25,000 regardless of the weaker year, so taxable income is 40,000 - 25,000 = $15,000; after 40% tax, net income is $9,000, and ROE is 9,000/500,000 = 1.8% -- far below the prior boom-year ROE, since operating return has now fallen below the after-tax cost of debt and leverage works against shareholders instead of for them. The 4.8% figure forgets to deduct the $25,000 interest expense before applying tax, and 3.0% applies no tax at all to the $15,000 taxable income, treating pre-tax income as if it were already net income.'),
  ('CI37', 'Corporate Issuers', 'Capital Structure', 'Recapitalization effect on ROE', 'A company currently has an all-equity capital structure with $2,000,000 of equity and generates $300,000 in operating income (EBIT), taxed at 30%. Management proposes replacing $800,000 of equity with $800,000 of new debt carrying a 6% interest rate, leaving $1,200,000 of equity, with EBIT unchanged. After this recapitalization, the company''s ROE is closest to:', '["14.7%","17.5%","8.8%"]'::jsonb, 0, 'New interest expense is 800,000 x 6% = $48,000, so taxable income falls to 300,000 - 48,000 = $252,000; after 30% tax, net income is $176,400, and ROE on the new, smaller $1,200,000 equity base is 176,400/1,200,000 = 14.7%. The 17.5% figure forgets to deduct the new $48,000 interest expense before applying tax, and 8.8% correctly computes the new $176,400 net income but divides it by the original $2,000,000 equity base instead of the reduced $1,200,000 post-recapitalization equity.'),
  ('CI38', 'Corporate Issuers', 'Working Capital Management', 'Sources of short-term liquidity', 'Which of the following is most accurately classified as a primary source of short-term liquidity for a company in the normal course of business?', '["Cash collections from customers and the available balance on an established, committed bank line of credit","Liquidating core long-term operating assets at a discounted price","Filing for court-supervised debt restructuring"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Primary sources of liquidity are the ones a company draws on routinely and without signaling distress: cash generated from operations, cash balances, short-term investments, and credit lines already arranged with lenders. Selling off core long-term assets at a discount and formal debt restructuring are both secondary sources -- options a company turns to only when its normal, primary sources have proven insufficient, and both typically signal financial trouble to the market.'),
  ('CI39', 'Corporate Issuers', 'Working Capital Management', 'Primary vs secondary liquidity', 'Compared with primary sources of liquidity, secondary sources of liquidity (such as renegotiating debt agreements or liquidating assets) are generally:', '["Less costly and used more routinely in the normal course of business","More costly and often signal that the company is already in financial difficulty","Identical in cost and availability, since both are always accessible on demand"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Secondary sources of liquidity are typically more expensive to use and are turned to only after primary sources (operating cash flow, cash balances, trade credit, committed credit lines) prove insufficient, so relying on them often signals to the market that the company is already under financial strain. Describing them as less costly and routine actually describes primary sources instead, and secondary sources are far from equally available on demand -- creditors and asset buyers may be unwilling to transact on favorable terms with a company that is visibly under stress.'),
  ('CI40', 'Corporate Issuers', 'Working Capital Management', 'Cash conversion cycle direction', 'A retailer negotiates more generous payment terms with its suppliers, increasing its days payable outstanding (DPO), while its days of inventory on hand and days of sales outstanding remain unchanged. This change will most likely cause the retailer''s cash conversion cycle to:', '["Increase, because a longer DPO adds directly to the length of the cash conversion cycle","Decrease, because DPO is subtracted in the cash conversion cycle formula and a longer DPO reduces the time cash is tied up","Stay the same, because DPO does not appear in the cash conversion cycle formula"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The cash conversion cycle equals days of inventory on hand plus days of sales outstanding minus days payable outstanding, so a longer DPO, taking more time to pay suppliers, is subtracted more heavily and shortens the cycle, freeing up cash for longer before it must go out the door. Describing a longer DPO as adding to the cycle reverses the sign of its role in the formula, and DPO is very much one of the three components of the cash conversion cycle, not an unrelated figure.'),
  ('CI41', 'Corporate Issuers', 'Working Capital Management', 'Working capital and profitability', 'A company holds significantly more inventory and offers much longer customer payment terms than it needs to operate smoothly, well beyond what sales volumes require. This excess investment in working capital most directly tends to:', '["Improve the company''s liquidity at no cost, since more inventory and receivables are always better for a company''s cash position","Reduce the return earned on the capital tied up in working capital, since that capital could otherwise be invested elsewhere","Have no effect on profitability, since working capital appears only on the balance sheet"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Capital tied up in excess inventory or receivables is not earning a return elsewhere in the business, so holding more working capital than operations require reduces the company''s overall return on invested capital even as it may cushion liquidity -- the core liquidity-versus-profitability trade-off in working capital management. Excess inventory and receivables are not free; they tie up cash and carry their own holding and financing costs, and working capital levels flow directly into profitability through financing costs and lost investment opportunities, not just onto the balance sheet in isolation.'),
  ('CI42', 'Corporate Issuers', 'Working Capital Management', 'Cash conversion cycle', 'A company has days of inventory on hand (DOH) of 60 days, days of sales outstanding (DSO) of 45 days, and days payable outstanding (DPO) of 30 days. The company''s cash conversion cycle is closest to:', '["135 days","45 days","75 days"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The cash conversion cycle equals DOH + DSO - DPO = 60 + 45 - 30 = 75 days. The 135-day figure simply adds all three components instead of subtracting DPO, treating the payables period as if it lengthened rather than shortened the cycle, and 45 days comes from swapping the roles of DSO and DPO in the formula (DOH + DPO - DSO), subtracting the wrong one of the two.'),
  ('CI43', 'Corporate Issuers', 'Working Capital Management', 'Deriving DSO for CCC', 'A company has average accounts receivable of $150,000 and annual credit sales of $1,825,000 (assume a 365-day year). Its days of inventory on hand is 40 days and its days payable outstanding is 35 days. The company''s cash conversion cycle is closest to:', '["35 days","70 days","25 days"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Days of sales outstanding is average receivables divided by credit sales per day: 150,000 / (1,825,000/365) = 150,000/5,000 = 30 days, so the cash conversion cycle is 40 + 30 - 35 = 35 days. The 70-day figure correctly derives DSO as 30 days but then forgets to subtract DPO at all (40 + 30), and 25 days subtracts the wrong component, computing DSO + DPO - DOH (30 + 35 - 40) instead of DOH + DSO - DPO.'),
  ('CI44', 'Corporate Issuers', 'Working Capital Management', 'Deriving DPO for CCC', 'A company''s days of inventory on hand is 50 days and its days of sales outstanding is 40 days. Its average accounts payable is $80,000 and its annual cost of goods sold is $912,500 (assume a 365-day year). The company''s cash conversion cycle is closest to:', '["58 days","90 days","122 days"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Days payable outstanding is average payables divided by purchases (here, cost of goods sold) per day: 80,000 / (912,500/365) = 80,000/2,500 = 32 days, so the cash conversion cycle is 50 + 40 - 32 = 58 days. The 90-day figure correctly derives DPO as 32 days but forgets to subtract it at all (50 + 40), and 122 days adds the correctly derived 32-day DPO instead of subtracting it (50 + 40 + 32).'),
  ('CI45', 'Corporate Issuers', 'Working Capital Management', 'Deriving DOH for CCC', 'A company''s average inventory is $200,000 and its annual cost of goods sold is $2,920,000 (assume a 365-day year). Its days of sales outstanding is 35 days and its days payable outstanding is 20 days. The company''s cash conversion cycle is closest to:', '["40 days","60 days","80 days"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Days of inventory on hand is average inventory divided by cost of goods sold per day: 200,000 / (2,920,000/365) = 200,000/8,000 = 25 days, so the cash conversion cycle is 25 + 35 - 20 = 40 days. The 60-day figure correctly derives DOH as 25 days but forgets to subtract DPO at all (25 + 35), and 80 days adds the correctly derived 25-day DOH, DSO, and DPO all together (25 + 35 + 20) instead of subtracting DPO.'),
  ('CI46', 'Corporate Issuers', 'Agency Theory', 'Information asymmetry', 'The principal-agent problem between shareholders and managers arises largely because:', '["Managers and shareholders always want exactly the same things, so no conflict is possible","Shareholders always have better information about daily operations than the managers they hire","Managers typically have more information about the company''s operations and their own actions than shareholders do, and may act in their own interest using that information advantage"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Managers run the company day to day and naturally know more about its operations, risks, and their own effort and decisions than dispersed shareholders can observe, and this information asymmetry lets a manager pursue personal interests (perks, empire-building, reduced effort) that shareholders cannot easily detect or prevent. Shareholders and managers do not automatically share the same objectives -- that misalignment is the entire reason agency theory exists -- and it is managers, not shareholders, who hold the informational advantage in this relationship.'),
  ('CI47', 'Corporate Issuers', 'Agency Theory', 'Aligning manager incentives', 'Which of the following mechanisms is most directly intended to align the interests of managers with those of shareholders?', '["Compensating managers heavily with equity-based pay, such as stock or stock options, tied to the company''s share price performance","Paying managers a fixed salary with no link to company performance, to guarantee their independence","Prohibiting managers from ever owning any shares in the company they manage"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Tying a meaningful part of manager compensation to the company''s own share price gives managers a direct personal stake in the same outcome shareholders care about, reducing the incentive to pursue empire-building or excessive perks at shareholders'' expense. A purely fixed salary delinks pay from performance entirely, doing nothing to align incentives, and banning manager share ownership would remove one of the most direct tools available for aligning the two groups'' interests rather than protecting shareholders.'),
  ('CI48', 'Corporate Issuers', 'Agency Theory', 'Bond covenants', 'To help control the shareholder-creditor agency conflict, lenders commonly include covenants in bond agreements that:', '["Grant bondholders full voting control over the company''s board of directors","Restrict the company from taking on additional debt or paying excessive dividends beyond specified limits","Guarantee bondholders a share of any upside if the company''s equity value increases"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Covenants restricting additional borrowing or capping dividend payouts limit management and shareholders'' ability to shift value away from existing creditors after the debt is already priced and outstanding, directly addressing the shareholder-creditor conflict. Covenants do not typically hand bondholders board voting control, which would blur the line between debt and equity claims, and a fixed debt claim by design does not include a share of the company''s equity upside -- that asymmetry between fixed downside protection and no upside participation is exactly why creditors need covenants for protection in the first place.'),
  ('CI49', 'Corporate Issuers', 'Agency Theory', 'Residual loss', 'Even after a company implements monitoring mechanisms (such as audits and board oversight) and bonding mechanisms (such as contractual manager commitments), some loss in firm value from the imperfect alignment of manager and shareholder interests typically remains. This remaining loss is best described as:', '["Bonding costs, since it results directly from the bonding mechanisms themselves","Residual loss, the value lost because monitoring and bonding can reduce, but rarely eliminate, the misalignment between manager and shareholder interests","A monitoring cost, since it is measured in the same units as the cost of the audit"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Residual loss is the specific name for the agency cost that remains even after monitoring and bonding mechanisms are in place, since no realistic set of controls can perfectly align a manager''s actions with shareholders'' interests at zero cost. It is not a bonding cost (the cost of the bonding mechanism itself, such as a manager''s contractual commitment) or a monitoring cost (the cost of oversight, such as audit fees); it is the separate, residual value destruction that persists despite both of those efforts.'),
  ('CI50', 'Corporate Issuers', 'Agency Theory', 'Total agency costs', 'Shareholders of a firm spend $300,000 annually on monitoring mechanisms such as audits and board oversight, and $150,000 annually on bonding mechanisms such as manager performance contracts. The residual loss from manager decisions these mechanisms fail to fully correct is estimated at $250,000 annually. Total agency costs for the year are closest to:', '["$700,000","$450,000","$550,000"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Total agency costs are the sum of all three components: monitoring costs plus bonding costs plus residual loss, or 300,000 + 150,000 + 250,000 = $700,000. The $450,000 figure adds only monitoring and bonding costs (300,000 + 150,000), leaving out the $250,000 residual loss entirely, and $550,000 adds only monitoring costs and residual loss (300,000 + 250,000), leaving out the $150,000 in bonding costs -- both omit one of the three required components rather than using a wrong formula.');
